      "math_academy_id": "q-142962",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "Given that the random variables \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(4,2)\n$$\n\nare mutually independent, what is the distribution of $Y=5{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N({\\mu }_{1},{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}),\\,{X}_{2}∼N({\\mu }_{2},{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}),\\,{X}_{3}∼N({\\mu }_{3},{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}),\n$$\n\nand ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ are real constants,  then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y & ={a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3} \\\\  & ∼N({a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3},\\,{a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}∼N(3,1),\\,{X}_{2}∼N(-3,2),\\,{X}_{3}∼N(4,2),\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY=5{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={a}_{1}{\\mu }_{1}+{a}_{2}{\\mu }_{2}+{a}_{3}{\\mu }_{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =15+3-8 \\\\  & =10, \\\\ \\text{Var}[Y] & ={a}_{1}^{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{a}_{2}^{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}+{a}_{3}^{2}{\\sigma }_{3}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\cdot 1+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 2+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =25\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =25+2+8 \\\\  & =35.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y=5{X}_{1}-{X}_{2}-2{X}_{3} & ∼N(10,35).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
